1
00:00:00,896 --> 00:00:10,068
Welcome to North America episode two of three. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,732 --> 00:00:22,586
Eight countries run down the southern neck of the continent: Mexico first,
then the Central American chain from Belize on the Caribbean to Panama on the
Pacific.

3
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:26,665
Let's bring Mexico and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:28,871 --> 00:00:44,351
Mexico fills the southern part of the continent, between the United States to
the north and Guatemala and Belize to the south. Its long Pacific coast and
the Gulf of Mexico face each other across the narrow waist of Tehuantepec.

5
00:00:45,016 --> 00:01:09,784
Its name comes from the Mexica, the people we call the Aztecs, whose city
Tenochtitlan lies under Mexico City today. Maize, chillies and tomatoes were
first grown here, and chocolate was first made here. Mexico City, built on a
drained lake, is sinking, in places by tens of centimetres a year.

6
00:01:10,446 --> 00:01:20,256
Remember Mexico as the maize and chocolate homeland, narrowing to a waist at
Tehuantepec with the Pacific on one side and the Gulf on the other.

7
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,079
Let's bring Belize and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:36,956
Belize sits on the Caribbean coast between Mexico and Guatemala, a small
English-speaking country on the narrowest part of the Central American
isthmus.

9
00:01:37,614 --> 00:01:54,714
It is the only country in Central America whose official language is English.
Its barrier reef runs along the coast, and the Great Blue Hole, a sinkhole
more than three hundred metres across, drops away inside it.

10
00:01:55,372 --> 00:02:04,288
Remember Belize as the English-speaking exception on the Caribbean shore, with
the largest blue hole on Earth punched through its reef.

11
00:02:04,962 --> 00:02:09,142
Let's bring Guatemala and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:11,345 --> 00:02:24,998
Guatemala lies west of Honduras and north of El Salvador, reaching from the
Pacific to the highlands and the Caribbean lowlands, with Belize and Mexico
along its northern border.

13
00:02:25,659 --> 00:02:34,959
Tikal, in its northern jungle, was one of the greatest cities of the Maya.
More than twenty Mayan languages are still spoken here.

14
00:02:35,622 --> 00:02:46,117
Hold Guatemala as the Maya heartland: jungle ruins in the north, volcanoes
along the Pacific, and more than twenty Mayan languages still alive.

15
00:02:46,873 --> 00:02:51,180
Let's bring Honduras and its neighbours into view.

16
00:02:54,429 --> 00:03:07,560
Honduras is the largest country on the Central American isthmus, with a long
Caribbean coast to the north and a narrow Pacific outlet to the south, ringed
by Guatemala, El Salvador and Nicaragua.

17
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:21,444
Copán, in its west, was a Maya city famous for its carved stone stelae. The
writer O. Henry coined "banana republic" about it in 1904.

18
00:03:22,103 --> 00:03:32,204
Remember Honduras as the long coast-to-coast isthmus country: Maya stelae in
the west, and the phrase "banana republic" born there.

19
00:03:32,885 --> 00:03:37,018
Let's bring El Salvador and its neighbours into view.

20
00:03:39,508 --> 00:03:50,851
El Salvador is the smallest country in Central America, on the Pacific coast
between Guatemala and Honduras. It is the only one of the seven without a
Caribbean coast.

21
00:03:51,502 --> 00:04:01,011
Its name means "The Saviour", named for Christ. In 2021 it became the first
country to make bitcoin legal tender.

22
00:04:01,662 --> 00:04:10,869
Picture El Salvador as the narrow Pacific sliver: the smallest of the Central
American countries, and the only one with no Caribbean shore.

23
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:14,963
Let's bring Nicaragua and its neighbours into view.

24
00:04:17,137 --> 00:04:29,246
Nicaragua sits on the Caribbean coast with the Pacific on its southwest side,
between Honduras and Costa Rica. Its large Lake Nicaragua lies in the west of
the country.

25
00:04:29,896 --> 00:04:41,367
Lake Nicaragua is one of the few freshwater lakes with sharks, which swim up
the river from the sea. Rubén Darío, the poet who founded Spanish-language
modernism, was born here.

26
00:04:42,026 --> 00:04:50,815
Remember Nicaragua as the country with a Caribbean lake and a Pacific shore at
once, and with bull sharks swimming inland.

27
00:04:51,495 --> 00:04:55,164
Let's bring Costa Rica and its neighbours into view.

28
00:04:57,346 --> 00:05:07,841
Costa Rica lies on the Central American isthmus between Nicaragua and Panama,
with both the Caribbean and the Pacific inside a country only a hundred
kilometres wide.

29
00:05:08,495 --> 00:05:17,574
It abolished its army in 1948. About a quarter of its land is protected in
parks and reserves.

30
00:05:18,237 --> 00:05:27,571
Hold Costa Rica as the green country: two coasts, no standing army since 1948,
and a quarter of the land in protected reserves.

31
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:32,056
Let's bring Panama and its neighbours into view.

32
00:05:34,227 --> 00:05:46,034
Panama forms the narrowest link between North and South America, with Colombia
just past its southeastern border. Its canal joins the Atlantic and the
Pacific.

33
00:05:46,690 --> 00:06:00,854
The canal, opened in 1914, lets ships pass between the two oceans without
sailing around South America. Because the isthmus bends, there are places
where you can watch the sun rise over the Pacific.

34
00:06:01,515 --> 00:06:12,010
Remember Panama as the waist of the Americas: the canal crossing it, and a
sunrise on one sea while the evening is still on the other.

35
00:06:16,159 --> 00:06:34,120
Mexico is the large country at the north of the chain. Belize, Guatemala,
Honduras, El Salvador, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and Panama follow southward, most
of them narrow and touching both seas.

36
00:06:34,775 --> 00:06:49,613
Remember maize and chocolate, a language that came from a village, a blue
hole, Tikal, Copán, the Pacific sliver, bull sharks, a country without an
army, and the canal that joins two oceans.
